    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 47</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a>, about four years after their invasion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 60</span><span class="rf-text">The Iceni under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> burn the town</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">886</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> captures London from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1066</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> is crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1381</span><span class="rf-text">Wat Tyler's rebels enter the city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1666</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Fire destroys about 60 per cent of the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Stink leads to Bazalgette's sewer system</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-blitz">The Blitz</a> begins on 7 September</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/londinium"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/londinium.jpg" alt="Londinium" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Londinium_400_AD-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fremantleboy, Drallim (translation), CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>London stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">River Thames</a> in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, at what is described as the river's lowest bridging point. The Thames was once much broader and shallower, with extensive wetlands, and at high tide its shores reached five times their present width. Archaeologists have found traces of earlier activity on the south foreshore, where the now underground River Effra flows into the river: a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> structure dated to between 4800 and 4500 BCE, and the remains of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> bridge whose timbers were dated to between 1750 and 1285 BCE. Struck flint at the Fulham Palace site shows prehistoric activity from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and activity there ends in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, around 300 BCE.</p><p>No Iron Age town has been found. The archaeologist Leslie Wallace is quoted as saying that, because no late pre-Roman Iron Age settlements or significant domestic refuse have been found despite extensive excavation, arguments for a purely Roman foundation of London are now common and uncontroversial. Scattered Brythonic settlements existed in the area, and the name London came into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Old English and Welsh from Common Brythonic, probably from a lost Celtic form *Londonjon.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> as a civilian town about four years after their invasion of 43 CE, around 47 CE. In 60 or 61 CE the Iceni, led by their queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>, stormed the town and burnt it down. It was rebuilt as a planned Roman town and recovered after perhaps ten years.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;London&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, Early modern:</p><ul><li>Anthony David Mills, A Dictionary of London Place Names (Oxford University Press, 2001)</li><li>David Mills, A Dictionary of London Place Names (Oxford University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muscovy-company.jpg" alt="Muscovy Company" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuscovyCompany_Seal_1555.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> grew rapidly after its rebuilding. By its height in the 2nd century it had a population of around 60,000 and major public buildings: the largest basilica north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, temples, bath houses, an amphitheatre and a large fort for the garrison. Political instability and recession from the 3rd century led to a slow decline, and by the end of the 5th century the Roman city was practically abandoned.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement of Lundenwic, a trading settlement outside the walls along what is now the Strand, grew to a likely population of 10,000 to 12,000. By about 680 it was a major port again. After 886 life and trade moved back inside the old walls, and London grew slowly until a dramatic increase around 950. In the 11th century it was the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The population was somewhat more than 15,000 in 1100 and roughly 80,000 in 1300 by one estimate, and some 18,000 and nearly 100,000 by another.</p><p>In the 16th and 17th centuries trade expanded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and monopoly companies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscovy-company">Muscovy Company</a> (1555) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> (1600) were chartered in London. The population rose from an estimated 50,000 in 1530 to about 225,000 in 1605. In 1700 London handled 80 per cent of England's imports, 69 per cent of its exports and 86 per cent of its re-exports. It grew from 1 million people in 1800 to 6.7 million a century later, and its population reached a peak of 8.6 million in 1939.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;London&#8221; cites where it covers Accents, Economy, Finance:</p><ul><li>Clark Greg, The Making of a World City London 1991 to 2021 (Wiley, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror.jpg" alt="William the Conqueror" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_the_Conqueror_(TFA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Roman London formed part of the province of Britannia until the end of Roman rule in 410. Some claim that it replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a> as the capital in the 2nd century, though no surviving evidence shows it was ever the capital, and it is also said to have superseded Colchester as principal city in 100. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> London belonged first to the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, was part of the kingdom of the East Saxons by the early 7th century, and came under direct Mercian control in the 730s.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders sacked the city in 842 and 851, and the Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> wintered there in 871. The city stayed in Danish hands until 886, when forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> captured it and placed it under Ealdorman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-of-mercia">Æthelred of Mercia</a>. After Æthelred died in 911 it passed to Wessex. London resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> in 994 and was the last place to hold out in 1013 before it too submitted. Cnut besieged it in 1016, and after the Battle of Assandun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside">Edmund Ironside</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> north of the Thames to him.</p><p>After 1066 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William of Normandy</a> was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> and built the Tower of London. Government settled at Westminster from the 12th century, while the City of London kept its own administration under the Corporation. The Greater London Council replaced the old County of London in 1965, was abolished in 1986, and the Greater London Authority was created in 2000.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;London&#8221; cites where it covers Roman London, Anglo-Saxon and Viking-period London:</p><ul><li>Dominic Perring, Roman London (Routledge, 1991)</li><li>Anne Lancashire, London Civic Theatre: City Drama and Pageantry from Roman Times to 1558 (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>Alan Vince, London (Blackwell, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex.jpg" alt="Sæberht of Essex" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex">Sæberht of Essex</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebert_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Speed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> was ethnically diverse, with inhabitants from Britannia, continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Six of the traditional seven city gates, Ludgate, Newgate, Aldersgate, Cripplegate, Bishopsgate and Aldgate, are of Roman origin; Moorgate is medieval. The Roman wall, built between 180 and 225, was about 3 kilometres long, 6 metres high and 2.5 metres thick, and defined the City's perimeter for centuries.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellitus">Mellitus</a>, the first post-Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, arrived in 604 after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saeberht-of-essex">Sæberht of Essex</a> converted, and founded the first St Paul's Cathedral. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> arrived about 1070. They suffered violence in 1190, a punitive levy in 1224 and 500 deaths in 1264, and were expelled by Edward I in 1290. Medieval trade was organised in craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, which effectively controlled the city and elected the Lord Mayor, and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> more than half the area of London belonged to religious houses. Foreigners made up an estimated 4,000 of 100,000 residents by 1600, and met xenophobia that increased after the 1580s.</p><p>In the 17th century the nave of Old St Paul's was the daytime meeting place of Londoners, with merchants conducting business in its aisles. St Paul's Churchyard was the centre of the book trade. Shakespeare built the Globe Theatre in Southwark in 1599. Coffeehouses were places of debate in the 18th century, and Fleet Street became the centre of the press. Irish refugees of the Great Famine settled in the Victorian city, and at one point Catholic Irish made up about 20 per cent of the population.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;London&#8221; cites where it covers Architecture, Royal Observatory, Greenwich and learned societies, Leisure and entertainment, LGBTQ scene:</p><ul><li>Matt Cook, London and the Culture of Homosexuality, 1885–1914 (Cambridge Studies in Nineteenth-Century Literature and Culture) (Cambridge University Press, 2008)</li><li>Stephen Tow, London, Reign Over Me How England's Capital Built Classic Rock (Rowman &amp; Littlefield Publishers, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-blitz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-blitz.jpg" alt="The Blitz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-blitz">The Blitz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinkel_He_111_over_Wapping,_East_London.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">German Air Force photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval London was made of narrow, twisting streets and combustible <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and straw, and fire was a constant threat. A tornado struck on 17 October 1091, reportedly destroying over 600 houses and killing two people. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a> of 1381 rebels led by Wat Tyler entered the city, stormed the Tower and executed the Lord Chancellor, Archbishop Simon Sudbury; Tyler was stabbed to death by the Lord Mayor William Walworth at Smithfield. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> in the mid-14th century killed between nearly a third and at least half of the population, by different estimates.</p><p>The Great Plague of 1665 and 1666 killed between around 60,000 people, one fifth of the population, and some 100,000, by different estimates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-pepys">Samuel Pepys</a> wrote on 4 September 1665 that over 7,400 had died in one week. The Great Fire began on 2 September 1666 in a bakery in Pudding Lane and burned until Thursday. It destroyed about 60 per cent of the city, including Old St Paul's, 87 parish churches and 44 livery company halls, and at most 16 lives are believed lost.</p><p>Cholera killed 14,000 in 1848 and 6,000 in 1866. The Great Stink of 1858 preceded Joseph Bazalgette's sewers. The Gordon Riots of 1780 left 285 rioters dead. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airship">airships</a> killed around 700 people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-blitz">The Blitz</a> ran from 7 September 1940 to 10 May 1941, with 71 raids and over 18,000 tonnes of high explosive, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> bombing killed about 30,000 Londoners. The Great Smog of 1952 killed between 10,000 and 12,000 by modern estimates.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-wren"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-wren.jpg" alt="Christopher Wren" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-wren">Christopher Wren</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christopher_Wren_by_Godfrey_Kneller_1711.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Godfrey Kneller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Great Fire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-wren">Christopher Wren</a> rebuilt the ruined parish churches and designed the new St Paul's Cathedral, with its dome, which was the city's primary symbol for at least a century and a half. His plan for a new street layout was not carried out, and the rebuilt City generally followed the old street plan. The Monument commemorates the fire. The cathedral was completed in 1708 or 1710, by different reckonings.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-westminster">Palace of Westminster</a>, which grew from Westminster Hall begun in 1097, remain the setting of royal ceremony and Parliament. The Tower of London, begun by William, is one of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> in the city, with Kew Gardens, the Westminster site and Greenwich, where the Royal Observatory defines the prime meridian. The Roman wall still marks the line of the City's boundaries.</p><p>Bazalgette's sewers, more than 2,100 kilometres of tunnels and pipes, are still in use. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> of 1851 drew 6 million visitors. London was the world's largest city from about 1831 to 1925, and from the 1940s it became home to many immigrants, primarily from Commonwealth countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Today it is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the United Kingdom, with about nine million people.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;London&#8221; cites where it covers Early modern, Late modern and contemporary:</p><ul><li>Susan D. Pennybacker, Vision for London, 1889–1914 (Routledge, 2005)</li><li>Christopher Hibbert, The London Encyclopaedia (Pan Macmillan, 2010)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)<span>c. 300</span></a><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur<span>c. 1200</span></a><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal<span>1404</span></a><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi<span>c. 1550</span></a><a href="/songs/greensleeves">Greensleeves<span>1580</span></a><a href="/songs/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen<span>c. 1650</span></a><a href="/songs/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen<span>c. 1666</span></a><a href="/songs/the-british-grenadiers">The British Grenadiers<span>c. 1706</span></a><a href="/songs/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!<span>1740</span></a><a href="/songs/london-bridge-is-falling-down">London Bridge Is Falling Down<span>c. 1744</span></a><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King<span>1745</span></a><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful<span>1751</span></a><a href="/songs/amazing-grace">Amazing Grace<span>1773</span></a><a href="/songs/the-twelve-days-of-christmas">The Twelve Days of Christmas<span>1780</span></a><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser<span>1797</span></a><a href="/songs/twinkle-twinkle-little-star">Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star<span>1806</span></a><a href="/songs/the-star-spangled-banner">The Star-Spangled Banner<span>1814</span></a><a href="/songs/oh-shenandoah">Oh Shenandoah<span>c. 1820</span></a><a href="/songs/hymn-to-liberty">Hymn to Liberty<span>1823</span></a><a href="/songs/drunken-